alue":"UUID_RECORDED"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERRORED"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stores append with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedNextIndex"},{"type":"text","value":". That compare-and-swap boundary prevents\ntwo writers from committing conflicting event histories for the same run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"run-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run state is the small routing record beside the log. It answers questions like:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is the run queued, running, paused, finished, or errored?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which workflow version started this run?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which awaitables are currently outstanding?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is the run waiting for a signal?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is the run waiting for an approval?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is there a timer deadline?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replay still comes from the event log. Run state makes routing and wake-ups\nefficient. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitingFor"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" are convenient projections for\ntoday's one-pause-at-a-time engine. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaiting[]"},{"type":"text","value":" array is the forward-facing\nshape for future fan-out, race, or multiple outstanding wait primitives."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"timers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleep"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleepUntil"},{"type":"text","value":" pause the workflow on an internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__timer"},{"type":"text","value":"\nsignal. The runtime persists a timer row with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wakeAt"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":" claims due timers and delivers the internal signal:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.sweep({\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host only wakes the runtime. The store decides which timers are actually\ndue."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"schedules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schedules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registered schedules are materialized into schedule buckets:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"workflows: {\n  digest: {\n    load: async () => digestWorkflow,\n    schedules: [\n      {\n        id: 'digest-every-15m',\n        schedule: every.minutes(15),\n        overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n        input: { batchSize: 100 },\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"materializeWorkflowSchedules"},{"type":"text","value":" computes the due fire time and upserts the\nschedule record. The sweep then claims due buckets and starts deterministic run\nIDs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This avoids \"infinite sleep loop\" workflows for recurring jobs. Each scheduled\ntick is a fresh run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"leases","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leases"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leases let many workers safely share one store."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a worker wants to execute work, it claims the run, timer, or schedule\nbucket with:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leaseOwner"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leaseMs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"now"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the worker finishes, it releases the lease. If it crashes, another worker can\nclaim stale work after the lease expires."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use leases to reduce concurrent execution. Use idempotency to protect external\nside effects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"idempotency","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Idempotency"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow idempotency has several layers:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":": starting the same run twice should not create two executions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stepCtx.id"},{"type":"text","value":": use this as the idempotency key for external side effects."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signalId"},{"type":"text","value":": webhook retries should deliver the same signal once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approvalId"},{"type":"text","value":": approval retries should deliver the same approval once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"schedule bucket IDs: the same scheduled tick should start the same run."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not rely on cron providers to deliver exactly once. Assume duplicate delivery\nis possible."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"drizzle-postgres-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drizzle/Postgres adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Drizzle/Postgres adapter implements "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowExecutionStore"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  schema: 'public',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the package-owned migration during setup/deploy:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"psql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow owns the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_*"},{"type":"text","value":" table definitions, indexes, leases, timers,\nschedules, and compatibility expectations. Apps should not copy those tables\ninto their own Drizzle schema for normal runtime use."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ensureSchema()"},{"type":"text","value":" is still useful for local demos, tests, and explicit admin\nbootstrap scripts. Runtime sweeps and host adapters do not call it for you. A\nmissing table during "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":" means the deployed database has not been\nmigrated yet, not that the host adapter failed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can override table names:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  tables: {\n    runs: 'app_workflow_runs',\n    events: 'app_workflow_events',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default tables include runs, run states, event locks, events, timers, signal\ndeliveries, schedules, and schedule buckets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Future schema changes are versioned with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres"},{"type":"text","value":". Apply new package migrations as part\nof upgrading the store package. The runtime assumes the database schema is\ncompatible with the installed store adapter version."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first migration also creates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_schema_migrations"},{"type":"text","value":" and records the\napplied migration ID. Future store migrations will be published as additional\nnumbered SQL files and should be applied in order before the new adapter version\nhandles production traffic."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retention","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retention"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal runs usually remain for some period so:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"attach/read APIs can show final results"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"webhook retries can remain idempotent"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"debugging has a timeline"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"metrics and audits can be extracted"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retention policy belongs to the store or an admin job. A production store should\neventually expose cleanup helpers for terminal runs and old events."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production requirements"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A production-quality store should provide:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"atomic event append"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"atomic claim and lease operations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"due timer indexes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"due schedule bucket indexes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"idempotent signal and approval delivery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stale lease recovery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"migration-safe schema management"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"query APIs for dashboards and support tools"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Postgres can do all of this in one portable substrate, which is why it is the\nfirst serious store target."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"persistence","text":"Persistence","level":1},{"id":"two-store-layers","text":"Two store layers","level":2},{"id":"what-persists","text":"What persists","level":2},{"id":"append-only-event-log","text":"Append-only event log","level":2},{"id":"run-state","text":"Run state","level":2},{"id":"timers","text":"Timers","level":2},{"id":"schedules","text":"Schedules","level":2},{"id":"leases","text":"Leases","level":2},{"id":"idempotency","text":"Idempotency","level":2},{"id":"drizzle-postgres-adapter","text":"Drizzle/Postgres adapter","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Retention","level":2},{"id":"production-requirements","text":"Production requirements","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Persistence\n\nDurability is the difference between \"an async function\" and \"a workflow.\" In\nTanStack Workflow, durability lives in an explicit store contract.\n\nThe store is responsible for the facts that must survive process exits,\ndeployments, retries, and concurrent workers.\n\n## Two store layers\n\nThere are two related store shapes:\n\n| Store | Package | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `RunStore` | `@tanstack/workflow-core` | The low-level replay store used directly by `runWorkflow`. |\n| `WorkflowExecutionStore` | `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` | The production runtime store for runs, events, timers, schedules, signals, approvals, leases, and timelines. |\n\nUse `RunStore` when embedding the core engine yourself. Use\n`WorkflowExecutionStore` when using `defineWorkflowRuntime`.\n\n## What persists\n\nThe runtime store persists:\n\n- **Runs**: `runId`, `workflowId`, `workflowVersion`, status, input, output, and\n  error.\n- **Run state**: the replay metadata needed by the core engine, including\n  `awaiting[]` plus current single-wait projections for hosts.\n- **Events**: the append-only workflow event log.\n- **Timers**: due wake-ups for `ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil`.\n- **Signals**: delivered external events with idempotency.\n- **Approvals**: delivered approval decisions with idempotency.\n- **Schedules**: registered recurring workflow definitions.\n- **Schedule buckets**: due schedule ticks that can be claimed and started.\n- **Leases**: ownership records for active work and stale recovery.\n\nThe store does not persist JavaScript functions. It persists stable identifiers\nand data. Workflow code is loaded by runtime registrations.\n\n## Append-only event log\n\nThe event log is the source of truth for replay. Durable primitives append\nevents such as:\n\n- `STEP_FINISHED`\n- `STEP_FAILED`\n- `SIGNAL_AWAITED`\n- `SIGNAL_RESOLVED`\n- `APPROVAL_REQUESTED`\n- `APPROVAL_RESOLVED`\n- `NOW_RECORDED`\n- `UUID_RECORDED`\n- `RUN_FINISHED`\n- `RUN_ERRORED`\n\nStores append with `expectedNextIndex`. That compare-and-swap boundary prevents\ntwo writers from committing conflicting event histories for the same run.\n\n## Run state\n\nRun state is the small routing record beside the log. It answers questions like:\n\n- Is the run queued, running, paused, finished, or errored?\n- Which workflow version started this run?\n- Which awaitables are currently outstanding?\n- Is the run waiting for a signal?\n- Is the run waiting for an approval?\n- Is there a timer deadline?\n\nReplay still comes from the event log. Run state makes routing and wake-ups\nefficient. `waitingFor` and `pendingApproval` are convenient projections for\ntoday's one-pause-at-a-time engine. The `awaiting[]` array is the forward-facing\nshape for future fan-out, race, or multiple outstanding wait primitives.\n\n## Timers\n\n`ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil` pause the workflow on an internal `__timer`\nsignal. The runtime persists a timer row with `wakeAt`.\n\nLater, `runtime.sweep()` claims due timers and delivers the internal signal:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe host only wakes the runtime. The store decides which timers are actually\ndue.\n\n## Schedules\n\nRegistered schedules are materialized into schedule buckets:\n\n```ts\nworkflows: {\n  digest: {\n    load: async () => digestWorkflow,\n    schedules: [\n      {\n        id: 'digest-every-15m',\n        schedule: every.minutes(15),\n        overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n        input: { batchSize: 100 },\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`materializeWorkflowSchedules` computes the due fire time and upserts the\nschedule record. The sweep then claims due buckets and starts deterministic run\nIDs.\n\nThis avoids \"infinite sleep loop\" workflows for recurring jobs. Each scheduled\ntick is a fresh run.\n\n## Leases\n\nLeases let many workers safely share one store.\n\nWhen a worker wants to execute work, it claims the run, timer, or schedule\nbucket with:\n\n- `leaseOwner`\n- `leaseMs`\n- `now`\n\nIf the worker finishes, it releases the lease. If it crashes, another worker can\nclaim stale work after the lease expires.\n\nUse leases to reduce concurrent execution. Use idempotency to protect external\nside effects.\n\n## Idempotency\n\nWorkflow idempotency has several layers:\n\n- `runId`: starting the same run twice should not create two executions.\n- `stepCtx.id`: use this as the idempotency key for external side effects.\n- `signalId`: webhook retries should deliver the same signal once.\n- `approvalId`: approval retries should deliver the same approval once.\n- schedule bucket IDs: the same scheduled tick should start the same run.\n\nDo not rely on cron providers to deliver exactly once. Assume duplicate delivery\nis possible.\n\n## Drizzle/Postgres adapter\n\nThe Drizzle/Postgres adapter implements `WorkflowExecutionStore`:\n\n```ts\nimport { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  schema: 'public',\n})\n```\n\nApply the package-owned migration during setup/deploy:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nWorkflow owns the `workflow_*` table definitions, indexes, leases, timers,\nschedules, and compatibility expectations. Apps should not copy those tables\ninto their own Drizzle schema for normal runtime use.\n\n`ensureSchema()` is still useful for local demos, tests, and explicit admin\nbootstrap scripts. Runtime sweeps and host adapters do not call it for you. A\nmissing table during `runtime.sweep()` means the deployed database has not been\nmigrated yet, not that the host adapter failed.\n\nYou can override table names:\n\n```ts\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  tables: {\n    runs: 'app_workflow_runs',\n    events: 'app_workflow_events',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe default tables include runs, run states, event locks, events, timers, signal\ndeliveries, schedules, and schedule buckets.\n\nFuture schema changes are versioned with\n`@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres`. Apply new package migrations as part\nof upgrading the store package. The runtime assumes the database schema is\ncompatible with the installed store adapter version.\n\nThe first migration also creates `workflow_schema_migrations` and records the\napplied migration ID. Future store migrations will be published as additional\nnumbered SQL files and should be applied in order before the new adapter version\nhandles production traffic.\n\n## Retention\n\nTerminal runs usually remain for some period so:\n\n- attach/read APIs can show final results\n- webhook retries can remain idempotent\n- debugging has a timeline\n- metrics and audits can be extracted\n\nRetention policy belongs to the store or an admin job. A production store should\neventually expose cleanup helpers for terminal runs and old events.\n\n## Production requirements\n\nA production-quality store should provide:\n\n- atomic event append\n- atomic claim and lease operations\n- due timer indexes\n- due schedule bucket indexes\n- idempotent signal and approval delivery\n- stale lease recovery\n- migration-safe schema management\n- query APIs for dashboards and support tools\n\nPostgres can do all of this in one portable substrate, which is why it is the\nfirst serious store target.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: persistence\ntitle: Persistence\n---\n\n# Persistence\n\nDurability is the difference between \"an async function\" and \"a workflow.\" In\nTanStack Workflow, durability lives in an explicit store contract.\n\nThe store is responsible for the facts that must survive process exits,\ndeployments, retries, and concurrent workers.\n\n## Two store layers\n\nThere are two related store shapes:\n\n| Store | Package | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `RunStore` | `@tanstack/workflow-core` | The low-level replay store used directly by `runWorkflow`. |\n| `WorkflowExecutionStore` | `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` | The production runtime store for runs, events, timers, schedules, signals, approvals, leases, and timelines. |\n\nUse `RunStore` when embedding the core engine yourself. Use\n`WorkflowExecutionStore` when using `defineWorkflowRuntime`.\n\n## What persists\n\nThe runtime store persists:\n\n- **Runs**: `runId`, `workflowId`, `workflowVersion`, status, input, output, and\n  error.\n- **Run state**: the replay metadata needed by the core engine, including\n  `awaiting[]` plus current single-wait projections for hosts.\n- **Events**: the append-only workflow event log.\n- **Timers**: due wake-ups for `ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil`.\n- **Signals**: delivered external events with idempotency.\n- **Approvals**: delivered approval decisions with idempotency.\n- **Schedules**: registered recurring workflow definitions.\n- **Schedule buckets**: due schedule ticks that can be claimed and started.\n- **Leases**: ownership records for active work and stale recovery.\n\nThe store does not persist JavaScript functions. It persists stable identifiers\nand data. Workflow code is loaded by runtime registrations.\n\n## Append-only event log\n\nThe event log is the source of truth for replay. Durable primitives append\nevents such as:\n\n- `STEP_FINISHED`\n- `STEP_FAILED`\n- `SIGNAL_AWAITED`\n- `SIGNAL_RESOLVED`\n- `APPROVAL_REQUESTED`\n- `APPROVAL_RESOLVED`\n- `NOW_RECORDED`\n- `UUID_RECORDED`\n- `RUN_FINISHED`\n- `RUN_ERRORED`\n\nStores append with `expectedNextIndex`. That compare-and-swap boundary prevents\ntwo writers from committing conflicting event histories for the same run.\n\n## Run state\n\nRun state is the small routing record beside the log. It answers questions like:\n\n- Is the run queued, running, paused, finished, or errored?\n- Which workflow version started this run?\n- Which awaitables are currently outstanding?\n- Is the run waiting for a signal?\n- Is the run waiting for an approval?\n- Is there a timer deadline?\n\nReplay still comes from the event log. Run state makes routing and wake-ups\nefficient. `waitingFor` and `pendingApproval` are convenient projections for\ntoday's one-pause-at-a-time engine. The `awaiting[]` array is the forward-facing\nshape for future fan-out, race, or multiple outstanding wait primitives.\n\n## Timers\n\n`ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil` pause the workflow on an internal `__timer`\nsignal. The runtime persists a timer row with `wakeAt`.\n\nLater, `runtime.sweep()` claims due timers and delivers the internal signal:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe host only wakes the runtime. The store decides which timers are actually\ndue.\n\n## Schedules\n\nRegistered schedules are materialized into schedule buckets:\n\n```ts\nworkflows: {\n  digest: {\n    load: async () => digestWorkflow,\n    schedules: [\n      {\n        id: 'digest-every-15m',\n        schedule: every.minutes(15),\n        overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n        input: { batchSize: 100 },\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`materializeWorkflowSchedules` computes the due fire time and upserts the\nschedule record. The sweep then claims due buckets and starts deterministic run\nIDs.\n\nThis avoids \"infinite sleep loop\" workflows for recurring jobs. Each scheduled\ntick is a fresh run.\n\n## Leases\n\nLeases let many workers safely share one store.\n\nWhen a worker wants to execute work, it claims the run, timer, or schedule\nbucket with:\n\n- `leaseOwner`\n- `leaseMs`\n- `now`\n\nIf the worker finishes, it releases the lease. If it crashes, another worker can\nclaim stale work after the lease expires.\n\nUse leases to reduce concurrent execution. Use idempotency to protect external\nside effects.\n\n## Idempotency\n\nWorkflow idempotency has several layers:\n\n- `runId`: starting the same run twice should not create two executions.\n- `stepCtx.id`: use this as the idempotency key for external side effects.\n- `signalId`: webhook retries should deliver the same signal once.\n- `approvalId`: approval retries should deliver the same approval once.\n- schedule bucket IDs: the same scheduled tick should start the same run.\n\nDo not rely on cron providers to deliver exactly once. Assume duplicate delivery\nis possible.\n\n## Drizzle/Postgres adapter\n\nThe Drizzle/Postgres adapter implements `WorkflowExecutionStore`:\n\n```ts\nimport { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  schema: 'public',\n})\n```\n\nApply the package-owned migration during setup/deploy:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nWorkflow owns the `workflow_*` table definitions, indexes, leases, timers,\nschedules, and compatibility expectations. Apps should not copy those tables\ninto their own Drizzle schema for normal runtime use.\n\n`ensureSchema()` is still useful for local demos, tests, and explicit admin\nbootstrap scripts. Runtime sweeps and host adapters do not call it for you. A\nmissing table during `runtime.sweep()` means the deployed database has not been\nmigrated yet, not that the host adapter failed.\n\nYou can override table names:\n\n```ts\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({\n  db,\n  tables: {\n    runs: 'app_workflow_runs',\n    events: 'app_workflow_events',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe default tables include runs, run states, event locks, events, timers, signal\ndeliveries, schedules, and schedule buckets.\n\nFuture schema changes are versioned with\n`@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres`. Apply new package migrations as part\nof upgrading the store package. The runtime assumes the database schema is\ncompatible with the installed store adapter version.\n\nThe first migration also creates `workflow_schema_migrations` and records the\napplied migration ID. Future store migrations will be published as additional\nnumbered SQL files and should be applied in order before the new adapter version\nhandles production traffic.\n\n## Retention\n\nTerminal runs usually remain for some period so:\n\n- attach/read APIs can show final results\n- webhook retries can remain idempotent\n- debugging has a timeline\n- metrics and audits can be extracted\n\nRetention policy belongs to the store or an admin job. A production store should\neventually expose cleanup helpers for terminal runs and old events.\n\n## Production requirements\n\nA production-quality store should provide:\n\n- atomic event append\n- atomic claim and lease operations\n- due timer indexes\n- due schedule bucket indexes\n- idempotent signal and approval delivery\n- stale lease recovery\n- migration-safe schema management\n- query APIs for dashboards and support tools\n\nPostgres can do all of this in one portable substrate, which is why it is the\nfirst serious store target.\n"}
